{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XGFE53m38LVjW2Q4c1mCD5","type":"tool_result","content":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:601:> ⚓ **Worth Securing — the absent-component pattern.** An object can exist while nothing at all happens, if the component that would act on it is absent. The object is real; it's stored; `kubectl get` will show it to you. But an object is a *description*, and descriptions don't do anything by themselves. Something has to be watching for it and willing to act. Remember the phrase: **an object without its component does nothing.** You're going to meet it four more times in this book, and each time it will look like a completely different problem until you recognize it.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:1324:| **Absent-component pattern** | An object without its component does nothing. The description exists; nothing acts on it. |\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:286:6. **An object without its component does nothing.** You have met it at least twice: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision, and a Service whose selector matched no Pods.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:341:*You'll also acquire one rule that outlives this chapter: an object without its component does nothing. You'll use it in Chapter 13, in Chapter 17, and on things this book never gets to.*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:628:**An object without its component does nothing.**\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:641:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Chapter 3's phrase, verbatim, and worth writing on something you will see again: **an object without its component does nothing.** You have now personally met three instances: the LoadBalancer with no provider, the Service with no matching Pods, and this. Three sightings of the same light, and you stop calling it a coincidence and start calling it a landmark.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:730:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1354:> Chapter 3 gave you the sentence — *an object without its component does nothing* — and told you that you would meet it four more times. This is where that debt comes due in full.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1394:           An object without its component does nothing.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1574:A) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider to fulfill it (Ch 9 §3); a Service whose selector matches no Pods (Ch 9 §4); an Ingress with no Ingress controller (Ch 10 §3); a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy (Ch 10 §7).\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1575:B) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: an Ingress with no Ingress controller, and a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1577:D) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider; an Ingress with no controller; a NetworkPolicy on an unsupporting plugin; an Ingress whose `pathType` does not match the request path.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1802:*An object without its component does nothing.* Four instances so far:\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1856:| **The rule** | An object without its component does nothing. Four instances of the pattern so far. Ask: *what is watching this, and is it installed?* |\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:811:You do. The phrase is Chapter 3's, and Chapter 10 §3 named it as a pattern: **an object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1123:**An object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*. A StorageClass naming a CSI driver nobody deployed is not an exotic failure you have to imagine. It is the documented prerequisite, unmet. The claim sits unbound. The Pod sits `Pending`. `kubectl get storageclass` shows the class, healthy and correct, describing a capability that does not exist.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1473:A) The absent-component pattern — \"an object without its component does nothing\"; the claim remains unbound\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1630:You now hold all four pluggable interfaces, and with them a rule you can state without help: an object without its component does nothing. You have watched that rule fire at the Ingress controller, at the network plugin, at the provisioner, and at the CSI driver — four sightings, one light. Chapter 17 will collect all four interfaces in one place and ask you what they have in common; you already know.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f0b7fa2-24e7-485f-9ba0-7e639d285e38","uuid":"2b2d9251-c67c-46ee-9b6c-9a9037238d78","timestamp":"2026-08-31T14:57:21.202Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:601:> ⚓ **Worth Securing — the absent-component pattern.** An object can exist while nothing at all happens, if the component that would act on it is absent. The object is real; it's stored; `kubectl get` will show it to you. But an object is a *description*, and descriptions don't do anything by themselves. Something has to be watching for it and willing to act. Remember the phrase: **an object without its component does nothing.** You're going to meet it four more times in this book, and each time it will look like a completely different problem until you recognize it.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:1324:| **Absent-component pattern** | An object without its component does nothing. The description exists; nothing acts on it. |\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:286:6. **An object without its component does nothing.** You have met it at least twice: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision, and a Service whose selector matched no Pods.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:341:*You'll also acquire one rule that outlives this chapter: an object without its component does nothing. You'll use it in Chapter 13, in Chapter 17, and on things this book never gets to.*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:628:**An object without its component does nothing.**\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:641:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Chapter 3's phrase, verbatim, and worth writing on something you will see again: **an object without its component does nothing.** You have now personally met three instances: the LoadBalancer with no provider, the Service with no matching Pods, and this. Three sightings of the same light, and you stop calling it a coincidence and start calling it a landmark.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:730:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1354:> Chapter 3 gave you the sentence — *an object without its component does nothing* — and told you that you would meet it four more times. This is where that debt comes due in full.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1394:           An object without its component does nothing.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1574:A) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider to fulfill it (Ch 9 §3); a Service whose selector matches no Pods (Ch 9 §4); an Ingress with no Ingress controller (Ch 10 §3); a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy (Ch 10 §7).\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1575:B) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: an Ingress with no Ingress controller, and a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1577:D) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider; an Ingress with no controller; a NetworkPolicy on an unsupporting plugin; an Ingress whose `pathType` does not match the request path.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1802:*An object without its component does nothing.* Four instances so far:\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1856:| **The rule** | An object without its component does nothing. Four instances of the pattern so far. Ask: *what is watching this, and is it installed?* |\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:811:You do. The phrase is Chapter 3's, and Chapter 10 §3 named it as a pattern: **an object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1123:**An object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*. A StorageClass naming a CSI driver nobody deployed is not an exotic failure you have to imagine. It is the documented prerequisite, unmet. The claim sits unbound. The Pod sits `Pending`. `kubectl get storageclass` shows the class, healthy and correct, describing a capability that does not exist.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1473:A) The absent-component pattern — \"an object without its component does nothing\"; the claim remains unbound\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1630:You now hold all four pluggable interfaces, and with them a rule you can state without help: an object without its component does nothing. You have watched that rule fire at the Ingress controller, at the network plugin, at the provisioner, and at the CSI driver — four sightings, one light. Chapter 17 will collect all four interfaces in one place and ask you what they have in common; you already know.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
