{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TFtZSX1P6n5hxrqgsRrLtH","type":"tool_result","content":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1005:> **Name this shape, because you are going to meet it again.** *The object exists but nothing happens without the component.* An Ingress with no ingress controller is the same shape. `kubectl top` with no metrics-server is the same shape. A vertical autoscaler that isn't shipped by default is the same shape. It is not four gotchas. It is one rule with four instances, and the rule is that Kubernetes will happily accept a record of intent that nothing in the cluster is currently able to act on. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 — Ingress without an ingress controller]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 — `kubectl top` without metrics-server]*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1082:**Name the pattern, because you will retrieve it by name:** *the object exists but nothing happens without the component.* You will meet this exact shape at least three more times in this book. Each time, the first instinct will be \"something is misconfigured,\" and each time the answer will be \"something is not installed.\"\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:300:**8.** The object is created and nothing routes traffic. This is exactly the pattern Chapter 10 named: the API server accepts and stores the object, and no controller is watching for it, so it is a record of intent with nobody to honor it. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:1279:You have met this exact shape before. The book told you to remember it by name, and Chapter 10 built a section on it: **the object exists; nothing happens without the component.** An Ingress object on a cluster with no Ingress controller is accepted by the API server, stored in etcd, retrievable with `kubectl get`, and completely inert. The API is the contract. The controller is the implementation. Kubernetes ships the contract; somebody has to install the implementation.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:1281:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n=== count ===\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:4\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:3\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:3","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f0b7fa2-24e7-485f-9ba0-7e639d285e38","uuid":"6e98e36f-ecad-4018-99eb-ecb62317eeb1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T14:57:22.436Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1005:> **Name this shape, because you are going to meet it again.** *The object exists but nothing happens without the component.* An Ingress with no ingress controller is the same shape. `kubectl top` with no metrics-server is the same shape. A vertical autoscaler that isn't shipped by default is the same shape. It is not four gotchas. It is one rule with four instances, and the rule is that Kubernetes will happily accept a record of intent that nothing in the cluster is currently able to act on. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 — Ingress without an ingress controller]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 — `kubectl top` without metrics-server]*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1082:**Name the pattern, because you will retrieve it by name:** *the object exists but nothing happens without the component.* You will meet this exact shape at least three more times in this book. Each time, the first instinct will be \"something is misconfigured,\" and each time the answer will be \"something is not installed.\"\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:300:**8.** The object is created and nothing routes traffic. This is exactly the pattern Chapter 10 named: the API server accepts and stores the object, and no controller is watching for it, so it is a record of intent with nobody to honor it. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:1279:You have met this exact shape before. The book told you to remember it by name, and Chapter 10 built a section on it: **the object exists; nothing happens without the component.** An Ingress object on a cluster with no Ingress controller is accepted by the API server, stored in etcd, retrievable with `kubectl get`, and completely inert. The API is the contract. The controller is the implementation. Kubernetes ships the contract; somebody has to install the implementation.\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-13-when-the-cluster-won-t-answer.md:1281:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object exists; nothing happens without the component]*\n=== count ===\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:4\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:3\n/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:3","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
