{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EJp6gFHAqo4TUPHJE4rn1L","type":"tool_result","content":"1181\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/twelve-factor-app.md ===\n1182\t# Concept: The twelve-factor app\n1183\t\n1184\tA methodology for software delivered as a service. It \"uses declarative formats for setup\n1185\tautomation… has a clean contract with the underlying operating system… is suitable for\n1186\tdeployment on modern cloud platforms… minimizes divergence between development and\n1187\tproduction… and can scale up without significant changes to tooling, architecture, or\n1188\tdevelopment practices.\" [source: twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23]\n1189\t\n1190\tIt predates Kubernetes and describes Kubernetes exactly. Not coincidence and not prophecy:\n1191\tboth came out of the same problem — running many applications reliably on shared\n1192\tinfrastructure — and reached the same conclusions.\n1193\t\n1194\t## The twelve\n1195\t\n1196\tI Codebase · II Dependencies · III Config · IV Backing services · V Build/release/run ·\n1197\tVI Processes · VII Port binding · VIII Concurrency · IX Disposability · X Dev/prod parity ·\n1198\tXI Logs · XII Admin processes [source: twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23]\n1199\t\n1200\t## The sort that matters (fig 15.1)\n1201\t\n1202\t| The platform gives you this | The platform makes this easy | Still your application's problem |\n1203\t|---|---|---|\n1204\t| III, VI, VIII, IX, XI | IV, V, X | I, II, VII, XII |\n1205\t\n1206\tThe middle column is where most disappointment lives — Kubernetes makes dev/prod parity\n1207\t*achievable*, not automatic.\n1208\t\n1209\t## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n1210\t\n1211\t**The twelve-factor app is a set of constraints an application accepts in exchange for being\n1212\trun by a platform. Kubernetes implements the platform side — config injection, stateless\n1213\treplication, graceful termination, log collection. It cannot implement the application side.\n1214\tAn application that writes its own log files, keeps session state in memory, or takes ninety\n1215\tseconds to start is not made twelve-factor by being containerized.**\n1216\t\n1217\t## ⚠ Do not restore\n1218\t\n1219\tPublication date, authorship, and \"predates Kubernetes by three years\" were removed at\n1220\trevision for want of a source and must stay removed. twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23 begins at\n1221\t\"In the modern era, software is commonly delivered as a service\" and carries no provenance.\n1222\t\n1223\t## Related\n1224\t[[factor-iii-config-in-environment]] [[factor-vi-stateless-processes]]\n1225\t[[factor-ix-disposability]] [[factor-xi-logs-as-event-streams]] [[configmap]] [[pod-lifetime]]\n1226\tForward: Ch 17 §3 — several cloud-native characteristics are these factors under newer names.\n1227\t=== END WRITE ===\n1228\t\n1229\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/factor-iii-config-in-environment.md ===\n1230\t# Concept: Factor III — config in the environment\n1231\t\n1232\t\"An app's config is everything that is likely to vary between deploys (staging, production,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f0b7fa2-24e7-485f-9ba0-7e639d285e38","uuid":"af98def8-2e6c-4b95-9393-a78db3ada204","timestamp":"2026-08-31T15:00:44.576Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-15\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/twelve-factor-app.md ===\n# Concept: The twelve-factor app\n\nA methodology for software delivered as a service. It \"uses declarative formats for setup\nautomation… has a clean contract with the underlying operating system… is suitable for\ndeployment on modern cloud platforms… minimizes divergence between development and\nproduction… and can scale up without significant changes to tooling, architecture, or\ndevelopment practices.\" [source: twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23]\n\nIt predates Kubernetes and describes Kubernetes exactly. Not coincidence and not prophecy:\nboth came out of the same problem — running many applications reliably on shared\ninfrastructure — and reached the same conclusions.\n\n## The twelve\n\nI Codebase · II Dependencies · III Config · IV Backing services · V Build/release/run ·\nVI Processes · VII Port binding · VIII Concurrency · IX Disposability · X Dev/prod parity ·\nXI Logs · XII Admin processes [source: twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23]\n\n## The sort that matters (fig 15.1)\n\n| The platform gives you this | The platform makes this easy | Still your application's problem |\n|---|---|---|\n| III, VI, VIII, IX, XI | IV, V, X | I, II, VII, XII |\n\nThe middle column is where most disappointment lives — Kubernetes makes dev/prod parity\n*achievable*, not automatic.\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n**The twelve-factor app is a set of constraints an application accepts in exchange for being\nrun by a platform. Kubernetes implements the platform side — config injection, stateless\nreplication, graceful termination, log collection. It cannot implement the application side.\nAn application that writes its own log files, keeps session state in memory, or takes ninety\nseconds to start is not made twelve-factor by being containerized.**\n\n## ⚠ Do not restore\n\nPublication date, authorship, and \"predates Kubernetes by three years\" were removed at\nrevision for want of a source and must stay removed. twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23 begins at\n\"In the modern era, software is commonly delivered as a service\" and carries no provenance.\n\n## Related\n[[factor-iii-config-in-environment]] [[factor-vi-stateless-processes]]\n[[factor-ix-disposability]] [[factor-xi-logs-as-event-streams]] [[configmap]] [[pod-lifetime]]\nForward: Ch 17 §3 — several cloud-native characteristics are these factors under newer names.\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/factor-iii-config-in-environment.md ===\n# Concept: Factor III — config in the environment\n\n\"An app's config is everything that is likely to vary between deploys (staging, production,","numLines":52,"startLine":1181,"totalLines":3270}}}
